[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] played the 2000–01 season in the [[Scottish Premier League]]. [[Martin O'Neill]] became manager and [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] won a [[Treble (association football)|domestic treble]] of the three major Scottish trophies: the [[Scottish League Cup]],<ref name=CIS>{{cite news | title =Celtic clinch Cup with Larsson treble | publisher =BBC Sport | date =18 March 2001| url =https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/cis_ins_cup/1226569.stm | access-date =15 September 2007 }}</ref> the [[Scottish Premier League]] trophy<ref name=SPL>{{cite news | title =Parkhead parties again | work =BBC Sport| date =23 April 2001 | url =https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/scot_prem/1291588.stm| access-date =15 September 2007 }}</ref> and the [[Scottish Cup]].<ref name="Celtic lift cup to complete Treble">{{cite news | title =Celtic lift cup to complete Treble | publisher =BBC Sport| date =26 May 2001 | url =https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/scottish_cup/1343842.stm| access-date =15 September 2007 }}</ref>

==Review and events==
===Management===
Following the departure of [[John Barnes (footballer)|John Barnes]] in February 2000,<ref>{{cite news |title =Barnes forced out | publisher =BBC Sport | date =10 February 2000 | url =https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/scotland/637415.stm| access-date =15 September 2007 }}</ref> Martin O'Neill was appointed as manager of Celtic in June 2000.<ref>{{cite news | title =O'Neill unveiled as Celtic boss | publisher =BBC Sport | date =1 June 2000 | url =https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/773512.stm| access-date =15 September 2007 }}</ref> [[Kenny Dalglish]], who was director of football at Celtic, left the club soon after.<ref>{{cite news | title =Dalglish saddened by sacking| publisher =BBC Sport | date =30 June 2000 | url =https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/football/scottish_premier/813101.stm| access-date =15 September 2007}}</ref> [[John Robertson (footballer, born 1953)|John Robertson]] and [[Steve Walford]] followed O'Neill from [[Leicester City F.C.|Leicester City]] to be assistant manager and coach.<ref>{{cite news | title =Trio reunited at Celtic| publisher =BBC Sport | date =24 August 2000 | url =https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/l/leicester_city/893020.stm| access-date =15 September 2007}}</ref>

===League campaign===
Celtic started their league campaign by going undefeated in 16 games until a heavy defeat to [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]] in November 2000. They lost only two more games, both in May after the league title had been secured. They won the [[Scottish Premier League]] with a victory over [[St Mirren F.C.|St Mirren]] in April,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/scot_prem/1265666.stm|publisher=BBC Sport|date=7 April 2001|title=Celtic recapture league title|access-date=31 August 2018}}</ref> receiving the trophy two weeks later after a 1–0 home win over [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Hearts]],<ref name=SPL /> having finished 15 points clear of the runner-up, Rangers, with 97 points.<ref>{{cite news | title =Celtic then and now| publisher =BBC Sport|author=Colin Moffatt|date=6 April 2002 | url =https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/1911930.stm | access-date =31 August 2018}}</ref>

Striker [[Henrik Larsson]] scored 53 goals to set a new Scottish scoring record for a single season.<ref>{{cite news | title =Larsson becomes a 49-goal legend| publisher =BBC Sport | date =15 April 2001 | url =https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/1279224.stm| access-date =15 September 2007 }}</ref>

Key games were:<ref>{{cite news | title =The games that won the title| publisher =BBC Sport | date =7 April 2001 | url =https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/1261031.stm| access-date =15 September 2007 }}</ref>

* Dundee United 1-2 Celtic (30 July) - The opening game of the season which set the standard for the rest of the season
* Celtic 6-2 Rangers (27 August) - The biggest win over rivals Rangers since the Scottish League Cup Final in 1957
* Aberdeen 1-1 Celtic (1 October) - An eight-match winning start to the season ended at Aberdeen but a late equaliser secured a point for Celtic
* Hibernian 0-0 Celtic (29 November) - Celtic ground out a draw after a heavy defeat to Rangers the previous week threatened to derail their season
* Dundee 1-2 Celtic (10 December) - Celtic snatched all the points with a last minute winner despite being out-played for much of the match
* Celtic 1-0 Rangers (11 February) - A narrow win for Celtic was enough to prevent Rangers from resurrecting their title challenge
* Rangers 0-3 Celtic (29 April) - Celtic's first win at Ibrox for six and a half years

===Cup competitions===
Celtic beat [[Raith Rovers F.C.|Raith Rovers]], [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Hearts]] and [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]] on the way to the [[Scottish League Cup]] final at [[Hampden Park]] in March 2001. They won the trophy, beating [[Kilmarnock F.C.|Kilmarnock]] 3-0 through a second-half [[hat-trick]] by [[Henrik Larsson]] despite going down to ten men after [[Chris Sutton]] was sent off.<ref name=CIS />

In the [[Scottish Cup]], Celtic beat [[Stranraer F.C.|Stranraer]], [[Dunfermline Athletic F.C.|Dunfermline]] after a replay, [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Hearts]] and [[Dundee United F.C.|Dundee United]] to reach the final in May 2001, where they beat [[Hibernian F.C.|Hibernian]] 3–0 with a goal from [[Jackie McNamara]] and two goals from [[Henrik Larsson]] to complete a domestic treble.<ref name="Celtic lift cup to complete Treble"/>

===European campaign===
Celtic had qualified for the [[UEFA Cup]] and began their campaign with an 11-0 aggregate win over [[Jeunesse Esch]] from Luxembourg in August 2000.<ref>{{cite news | title =Celtic hit a magnificent seven| publisher =BBC Sport | date =24 August 2000 | url =https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/894988.stm| access-date =15 September 2007 }}</ref> A Chris Sutton goal in extra-time in the away leg was enough to see Celtic through the next round 3–2 on aggregate against [[HJK Helsinki]] the following month.<ref>{{cite news | title =Sutton is Celtic's saviour| publisher =BBC Sport | date =28 September 2000 | url =https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/uefa_cup/945675.stm| access-date =15 September 2007 }}</ref> They were knocked out in the third round of the competition by [[Girondins de Bordeaux]] 2–3 on aggregate despite dominating the second leg in Glasgow.<ref>{{cite news | title =Uefa Cup agony for Celtic| publisher =BBC Sport| date =9 November 2000 | url =https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/1015978.stm| access-date =15 September 2007}}</ref>

